            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Gujarati for to pester, then the Gujarati for to hope."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: હરખ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "હરખ — harakh — \"delight; joy at good news\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "દીકરાનું લગ્ન નક્કી થયું, તેથી દાદીને ઘણો હરખ થયો. dīkrānũ lagna nakkī thayũ, tethī dādīne ghaṇo harakh thayo. My grandmother was filled with delight when her son's wedding was fixed."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A masculine noun."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "હરખ (harakh) probably goes back to Sanskrit harṣa, \"joy, delight\", which Monier-Williams records; it is the everyday spoken shape of that learned word."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for describing what you lived and felt."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
